PlayableContent.put("game","final-fantasy",{"store":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2515020/FINAL_FANTASY_XVI/","verb":"command a party through whichever battle system this entry built: menu commands, an Active Time Battle gauge, programmable gambits, or real-time combos","first30":"Final Fantasy (1987) sets the formula in its first thirty seconds: the player picks four heroes from six classes, Fighter, Thief, Black Belt, Red Mage, White Mage or Black Mage, is placed on the world map beside the first castle town, and meets its first random encounter as a turn-based menu battle, four heroes lined up on the right and the enemies on the left, before any story has explained who these heroes are or why the crystals matter.","minute":"Which command, spell or gambit rule to give this character right now, inside whatever battle system this specific entry invented? Which job, licence, Crystarium node or Eikonic Ability is worth this game’s own currency, Ability Points, Licence Points, Crystarium Points, next? And, underneath every entry’s different answer: is this new, unfamiliar system worth learning from scratch again, or would the last game’s rules have solved this fight faster?","engines":["storytelling","progression","mastery","discovery"],"why":"Final Fantasy survives by rebuilding almost everything except its motifs. Each numbered entry invents a new cast, a new named world and, nearly every time, an entirely different way of fighting: Hiroyuki Ito’s Active Time Battle system in Final Fantasy IV (1991) gave way to the strictly turn-based Conditional Turn-Based system in Final Fantasy X (2001), to gambit-driven real-time battles in Final Fantasy XII (2006), and to real-time action in Final Fantasy XV (2016) and Final Fantasy XVI (2023). What survives every reinvention is a small, fixed kit of connective tissue: crystals or an equivalent power source in crisis, a character named Cid, most often an engineer, since Final Fantasy II (1988), chocobos and moogles, summonable creatures such as Ifrit and Bahamut, and two short pieces by Nobuo Uematsu: the Prelude, which opens most entries, and the Victory Fanfare, which closed battles in the first ten. A new player learns an unfamiliar system every time; a returning player recognises the franchise anyway.","complaints":"Discarding the previous game’s system every time means a favourite mechanic, gambits, the licence board, job juggling, is usually visited once and never returned to, and a new entry can alienate players who came to love exactly the system it just replaced. Final Fantasy XIII (2009) confined players to a single corridor for its first ten chapters, widely criticised as its biggest fault; Final Fantasy XV (2016) shipped after a decade of retooling with narrative gaps critics noted at launch and patched in through paid episodes released into 2019; and Final Fantasy XIV’s disastrous 2010 launch shows how badly the formula’s core habit, changing everything at once, can fail when applied to a live product rather than a single-purchase game.","lesson":"Rebuilding a series’ core system every entry keeps it from calcifying the way a series that never changes can, but it only works if the constants, here a handful of motifs and two pieces of music, do enough identity work on their own that players can recognise and trust the brand while every mechanic underneath it changes. Take away or half-execute either half of that trade, a stable identity or a genuinely new system, and the entry reads as either generic or unfinished rather than as a real reinvention.","misses":"Spin-offs and imitators that copy Final Fantasy’s surface motifs, crystals, chocobos, an engineer named Cid, without the discipline of actually rebuilding the underlying system each time end up as a static series wearing a reinventive one’s branding. Others copy the willingness to change everything, as the original Final Fantasy XIV did in 2010, without protecting the one thing a live product cannot afford to get wrong at launch, basic technical competence, mistaking constant reinvention for a licence to ship anything unfinished under a trusted name.","entries":[{"t":"Final Fantasy","short":"Formula set","platform":"NES","added":"Introduced a six-class hero system, world-saving crystals corrupted by four Fiends, and the turn-based menu battle template the series still answers to.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-i-pr.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy (1987), Pixel Remaster edition: four heroes face a group of Goblins in a turn-based menu battle on an open field.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1173770/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy IV","short":"Active Time Battle","platform":"Super Famicom","added":"Introduced the Active Time Battle system, where each character acts when a gauge fills, and gave every party member an unchangeable class tied to the story, so the party read as a cast of characters instead of blank job slots.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-e-iv.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy IV (1991), Pixel Remaster edition: Cecil’s party faces the armoured villain Golbez in a crystal-tiled room, the menu boxes at the bottom with each hero’s HP, MP and a yellow time gauge.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1173800/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy VI","short":"Ensemble finale","platform":"SNES","added":"Closed the sixteen-bit era with fourteen playable characters and no single protagonist, later ranked by IGN as the best entry in the whole series.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-vi-pr.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy VI (1994), Pixel Remaster edition: the party fights Ultros on the Opera House stage, with the Attack, Steal, Magic and Items battle menu at bottom left.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1173820/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy VII","short":"3D, Materia, huge","platform":"PlayStation","added":"Moved the series to 3D character models over pre-rendered backgrounds, introduced the swappable Materia system, and became its best-selling entry worldwide.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-vii-orig.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy VII (1997), the original game’s current Steam edition: Cloud and Barret battle a mechanical enemy, with HP, MP, Limit and Time gauges shown for each character.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/3837340/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy VIII","short":"Junction","platform":"PlayStation","added":"Removed magic points: spells are drawn from enemies, stocked in limited numbers and attached to stats such as Strength. It was also the first in the series to use realistically proportioned characters throughout, and it sold 2.21 million copies on its first day in Japan.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-e-viii.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy VIII (1999), PC edition: three party members face a winged red monster on a metal platform, the Attack, Magic, GF and Draw command menu at left and each hero’s HP with a gauge at right.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/39150/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy X","short":"Voiced, CTB","platform":"PlayStation 2","added":"Added full voice acting and continuous 3D areas, replacing the Active Time Battle gauge with the strictly turn-based Conditional Turn-Based system.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-x-hd.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy X/X-2 HD Remaster: Tidus looks out over the ruins of Zanarkand at sunset.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/359870/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy XII","short":"Gambits","platform":"PlayStation 2","ref":"final-fantasy-xii","added":"Added programmable gambits that issue party commands automatically inside a seamless real-time field; analysed in full on its own page in this library.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-xii-entry.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy XII The Zodiac Age: Fran, Basch and Ashe fight a giant armoured boss beside a pool of water, each party member’s next action listed by name at the bottom right.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/595520/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy XIII","short":"Corridor, Paradigm","platform":"PlayStation 3, Xbox 360","added":"Traded open towns for a single corridor across its first ten chapters and the fast Paradigm role-switching battle system, splitting critics.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-xiii-shot.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy XIII (2009): Lightning rides Odin, her Eidolon, in its horse-like Gestalt form during a fiery battle.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/292120/","licence":"store"}}},{"shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-xiv-1-0.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy XIV (2010, version 1.0, before A Realm Reborn): the player character aims a matchlock arquebus at an NPC on a city street, the dialogue log open below.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://www.pcgamer.com/final-fantasy-xiv-review/","licence":"press","source":"PC Gamer"}},"t":"Final Fantasy XIV","short":"Launch, rebirth","platform":"Windows; PS3 from 2013","added":"Launched as a broken subscription MMO in 2010, then was rebuilt entirely from scratch as A Realm Reborn in 2013, saving the series’ live-service model."},{"t":"Final Fantasy XV","short":"10-year dev","platform":"PlayStation 4, Xbox One","added":"Emerged after a decade of development as an open-world road trip with real-time combat, its story finished later through paid episodes.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-xv-roadtrip.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy XV Windows Edition: the Regalia convertible drives through a palm-lined town on the game’s open-world road trip.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/637650/","licence":"store"}}},{"t":"Final Fantasy XVI","short":"Full action","platform":"PlayStation 5","added":"Became what Square Enix calls the series’ first fully fledged action RPG: one controllable hero, with combat built by a former Devil May Cry designer.","shot":{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-xvi-eikon.webp","alt":"Final Fantasy XVI (2023): Ifrit, the Dark Eikon, rises through smoke and embers beneath the moon.","credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2515020/","licence":"store"}}}],"constant":"What survives every rebuild is a small, fixed kit: a magical power source, crystals in the earliest entries and Mothercrystals in the most recent one, whose corruption or overuse drives the plot; a new cast and named world introduced from zero rather than continued from the last game; and a handful of recurring names and creatures, a character called Cid, most often an engineer, since Final Fantasy II (1988), chocobos in every mainline entry since that same game, moogles, and summonable beings such as Ifrit, Shiva and Bahamut. Nobuo Uematsu’s Prelude has opened most entries since 1987, and his Victory Fanfare closed battles in the first ten. None of this constant says anything about how battles are actually played, which is precisely the point.","changed":"What renews the format is the battle system itself, rebuilt almost every generation: the Active Time Battle system (Final Fantasy IV, 1991) gave way to the strictly turn-based Conditional Turn-Based system (Final Fantasy X, 2001), then to gambit-driven real-time fields (Final Fantasy XII, 2006), then to real-time action (Final Fantasy XV, 2016) and a single-hero action system with no turns at all (Final Fantasy XVI, 2023). What stalled the format was change applied to the wrong layer: Final Fantasy XIV’s 2010 launch changed genre, engine and business model simultaneously while getting server performance and basic MMO design wrong, drawing a 49 out of 100 Metacritic score and forcing Square Enix to rebuild it from scratch as A Realm Reborn (2013) rather than patch it forward.","reception":[{"entry":"Final Fantasy VI","year":1994,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"Electronic Gaming Monthly’s four reviewers each scored it 9 out of 10 and Game Informer gave it 9.5 out of 10; IGN’s 2007 Top 100 list ranked it the ninth best game of all time, above every other Final Fantasy, and it had shipped 3.48 million copies worldwide by March 2003.","why":"Where earlier entries built their story around one or two heroes, Final Fantasy VI gave its fourteen playable characters equal narrative weight under the stated design idea that every character is the protagonist, and let its non-linear second half, the World of Ruin, be explored with whichever cast the player had grown attached to; critics and retrospectives rewarded that structural risk rather than any single new battle mechanic.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_VI"]},{"entry":"Final Fantasy XIII","year":2009,"verdict":"mixed","evidence":"Final Fantasy XIII scored 83 on Metacritic for PlayStation 3 and 82 for Xbox 360, respectable aggregates, but Edge gave it only 5 out of 10 and 1UP.com called its linearity the “biggest shortcoming”, with GamePro describing progression as little more than “a long hallway toward an orange target symbol on your mini-map”.","why":"Rather than the towns and open fields earlier entries let players wander from the start, Final Fantasy XIII confines the party to a single corridor for its first ten chapters on Cocoon, with few towns, before Gran Pulse opens into a traditional open area. Director Motomu Toriyama blamed reviewers who approached it from a Western point of view, saying it “becomes very difficult to tell a compelling story when you’re given that much freedom”. The split itself, strong aggregates beside a repeated linearity complaint while Edge called the battle system “among the genre’s finest”, suggests the corridor, not the Paradigm system, divided critics.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XIII"]},{"entry":"Final Fantasy XIV","year":2010,"verdict":"panned","evidence":"The original release scored 49 out of 100 on Metacritic from 26 reviews; IGN gave it 5.5 out of 10, GameSpot 4 out of 10 calling it “a notable entry to the genre but only for what it lacks”, GameTrailers 4.2 out of 10 describing it as “[a] broken, incomplete mess” released “before it was finished”, and PC Gamer scored it 30 percent.","why":"Square Enix moved a numbered Final Fantasy into a genre, the subscription MMO, it had attempted only once before, on the Crystal Tools engine built for Final Fantasy XIII, with a team told merely to make something “different from Final Fantasy XI”. Producer Naoki Yoshida’s 2014 GDC postmortem named three causes: an over-emphasis on graphical quality at the expense of server performance, a team without modern MMO expertise, and a belief that launch problems could be patched later. President Yoichi Wada apologised in December 2010, and in September 2011 said the launch had “greatly damaged” the Final Fantasy brand.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XIV_(2010_video_game)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XIV"]},{"entry":"Final Fantasy XIV: A Realm Reborn","year":2013,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"A Realm Reborn scored 83 on Metacritic for PC, 78 for PlayStation 3 and later 86 for PlayStation 4, against Famitsu’s 39 out of 40; the PlayStation 3 version placed second in Japan’s sales chart in its first week with 184,000 physical copies sold, registrations passed 1.5 million by late October 2013, and the game reached over 24 million registered players by its fourth expansion, becoming the most profitable entry in the series.","why":"Rather than patch the failed 2010 release, producer and director Naoki Yoshida shut the original version down entirely in November 2012 and rebuilt it from scratch on a new engine and server infrastructure starting in 2011, fixing the specific faults he later named at the 2014 Game Developers Conference: server performance sacrificed for graphics, a team that lacked modern MMORPG expertise, and a belief that patches alone could fix launch problems; Yoshida also began publishing “Letters from the Producer” to rebuild player trust, saying sales were secondary compared with redeeming the reputation of the series.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XIV"]},{"entry":"Final Fantasy XV","year":2016,"verdict":"mixed","evidence":"Final Fantasy XV scored 81 on Metacritic for PlayStation 4, 83 for Xbox One and 85 for the PC Windows Edition and shipped five million copies on its first day, but opinions on its story were mixed: Hardcore Gamer’s Adam Beck called the main story a “monumental disappointment”. Square Enix added Episodes Gladiolus, Prompto and Ignis in 2017 and Episode Ardyn in 2019, story content meant to answer criticism of the narrative’s missing details.","why":"Announced in 2006 as Final Fantasy Versus XIII and made a mainline numbered entry in 2012, announced at E3 2013, when director Hajime Tabata replaced Tetsuya Nomura, the game carried roughly a decade of retooling into its final open-world road-trip design; reviewers who praised its central four-friends road trip and real-time combat also noted narrative threads that only made full sense once the post-launch episodes filled them in, arguably a story cut down to fit, since the change of director had forced a rewrite in which some scenes and characters were repurposed or removed.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XV"]},{"entry":"Final Fantasy XVI","year":2023,"verdict":"praised","evidence":"Final Fantasy XVI scored 87 on Metacritic for PlayStation 5, 84 for PC and 88 for Xbox Series X/S; Game Informer called it the reviewer’s “favorite action combat system to date”, “fast and easy to understand on its surface” but with “a lot of depth”, and it sold over three million copies in its first week, though Square Enix said the game “did not meet the company’s higher expectations” despite those numbers.","why":"Producer Naoki Yoshida surveyed the fanbase, found it split between action and turn-based preferences, and chose action anyway, arguing that pleasing everyone would “run the risk of creating a half-baked compromise of a game”. Final Fantasy XV had already gone real-time; XVI went further, with one controllable hero and combat built by battle designer Ryota Suzuki, a Capcom veteran of Devil May Cry. GameSpot said it “perhaps marks the series’ most drastic shift yet”, and critics praised the combat while faulting its lack of mechanical depth, so the decision that won reviewers also thinned the RPG systems around it.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XVI","https://gameinformer.com/review/final-fantasy-xvi/victory-in-the-land-of-gods-and-monsters","https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/final-fantasy-16-review-on-its-own-terms/1900-6418081/"]}],"receptionLesson":"Across these six verdicts, critics rarely punished the new battle system itself. Edge called Final Fantasy XIII’s battle system “among the genre’s finest” and still gave the game 5 out of 10 for its corridor; reviewers of the 2010 Final Fantasy XIV praised the concepts behind its job and levelling systems while condemning its pace, interface and bugs; Final Fantasy XV’s combat was praised for its pace while its story split critics; Final Fantasy XVI’s combat won reviewers over while side quests and thin RPG systems drew the complaints. Verdicts moved with everything built around the fights: towns, pacing, interface, story and servers. Final Fantasy VI was rewarded for its cast and its open second half, and A Realm Reborn for rebuilding the whole game around a class system it kept from 2010. A team that rebuilds its core system should budget to rebuild that surrounding structure too, because that is where the reviews land.","diagrams":[{"title":"Final Fantasy: the entries that shaped it","steps":[{"id":"e0","t":"Formula set","d":"1987 · NES"},{"id":"e1","t":"Active Time Battle","d":"1991 · Super Famicom"},{"id":"e2","t":"Ensemble finale","d":"1994 · SNES"},{"id":"e3","t":"3D, Materia, huge","d":"1997 · PlayStation"},{"id":"e4","t":"Junction","d":"1999 · PlayStation"},{"id":"e5","t":"Voiced, CTB","d":"2001 · PlayStation 2"},{"id":"e6","t":"Gambits","d":"2006 · PlayStation 2"},{"id":"e7","t":"Corridor, Paradigm","d":"2009 · PlayStation 3, Xbox 360"},{"id":"e8","t":"Launch, rebirth","d":"2010 · Windows; PS3 from 2013"},{"id":"e9","t":"10-year dev","d":"2016 · PlayStation 4, Xbox One"},{"id":"e10","t":"Full action","d":"2023 · PlayStation 5"}],"edges":[["e0","e1"],["e1","e2"],["e2","e3"],["e3","e4"],["e4","e5"],["e5","e6"],["e6","e7"],["e7","e8"],["e8","e9"],["e9","e10"]]},{"title":"Final Fantasy: new world, same crisis","steps":[{"t":"New cast, world","d":"a new hero, cast and setting"},{"t":"Learn the system","d":"this entry’s own battle rules"},{"t":"Grow","d":"spend this game’s own currency"},{"t":"Face the crisis","d":"a power tied to crystals fails"},{"t":"Save the world","d":"until the next game resets it"}]}],"signature":{"mechanism":"Every mainline entry starts from zero: a new cast with no returning protagonist, a new named world, and, almost every time, a different battle system entirely. Final Fantasy (1987) resolves turns from a menu; Final Fantasy IV (1991) adds Hiroyuki Ito’s Active Time Battle system, letting speed decide who acts and how often; Final Fantasy X (2001) drops that gauge for the strictly turn-based Conditional Turn-Based system; Final Fantasy XII (2006) adds programmable gambits that issue those menu commands automatically in a seamless field; Final Fantasy XVI (2023) removes party control for real-time combat built by a former Devil May Cry designer. What survives every rebuild is a fixed kit of connective tissue: crystals or an equivalent power source in crisis, an engineer named Cid, chocobos, moogles, and summonable creatures such as Ifrit and Bahamut, so a player who has never seen this entry’s rules still recognises it as a Final Fantasy game within its first hour.","teach":"A new entry teaches its own rules from zero because it must: with no returning mechanic to lean on, each Final Fantasy puts the player into a fight using only that game’s own vocabulary, Final Fantasy V’s Active Time Battle gauge, the first visible one, shown filling beside each name, Final Fantasy XII’s gambit list starting empty so the player fights by hand first, Final Fantasy XVI’s cooldown icons appearing the moment Clive gains his first Eikonic ability. The recurring motifs do the opposite job: a crystal, a chocobo, or a character introduced as Cid tells a veteran player, before the tutorial has finished, roughly what kind of story and world they have entered, using recognition rather than instruction.","fair":"Because the mechanical rules reset each time, fairness is rebuilt rather than inherited: Final Fantasy X’s Conditional Turn-Based system pauses on each player turn and shows a timeline of who acts next, so a player new to its rules can plan without time pressure. What stays constant is a softer safety net: a lost fight sends the player back to a save or checkpoint, and no entry deletes the save file, so experimenting with an unfamiliar system, gambits, Eikonic Feats, the Paradigm system, costs time rather than the whole run. A player who misjudges Final Fantasy XVI’s cooldown timing loses a fight, not a character.","escalate":"Escalation happens on two clocks at once. Within a game, later fights demand more of whatever that entry’s system offers: harder weakness reads in Final Fantasy XVI’s Eikon clashes, deeper investment in Final Fantasy XIII’s Crystarium. Across the series, escalation is generational: Final Fantasy VII (1997) escalated presentation with pre-rendered 3D backgrounds and CGI; Final Fantasy XI (2002) escalated the business model into the series’ first monthly subscription; Final Fantasy XV (2016) escalated scope into a decade of development after starting life as Versus XIII in 2006. Each escalation risks the format itself, since a bigger or more experimental entry can fail to ship the exact thing its predecessor got right.","copies":"The formula only works if the studio is willing to throw its own successful system away on schedule. Bandai Namco’s Tales series copies only half of it: its main entries “stand independent of each other with different characters and stories”, but the Linear Motion Battle System is its “main unifying element”, changed incrementally between entries. That keeps a battle system players already trust, and gives up the renewal, and the risk, that Final Fantasy buys by rebuilding the fight itself. Two other copies fail in other ways. A clone that keeps the motifs (a crystal, an engineer called Cid, chocobos) but reuses one battle system every time has nothing new under the recognition, so entries feel like reskins. A clone that rebuilds the fight every time but drops the motifs loses the signal that tells a returning player which series they have entered.","prototype":"Grey-box a single battle screen with a placeholder cast and one objective: defeat an enemy group. Build it around exactly one resource-and-turn rule of your own invention, not a copy of an existing RPG’s system, and ship it as a complete two-hour vertical slice with its own opening scene, its own recurring named engineer character, and one recognisable creature design. Then, without keeping a single line of that battle system’s code, build a second vertical slice with a different cast, a different world and a different battle rule, keeping only the engineer’s name and the creature. Compare which slice still feels like the same series to a player who tries both, and ask what carried that feeling if the mechanics did not."},"lens":{"gameplay":{"claim":"Whatever the battle system, Final Fantasy keeps one shared spectacle move, summoning a giant creature for a huge attack, and the series’ real argument across nearly four decades is over what that move should cost.","evidence":"Final Fantasy VII (1997) treats summons as Materia, equippable orbs any character can cast for a magic cost once found. Final Fantasy XV (2016) removes control over when a summon appears: its Astral summons, in RPGSite’s words, “have minds of their own and only show up under certain circumstances”, appearing at random when conditions such as allies being downed or Noctis in Danger status are met. Final Fantasy XVI (2023) turns the idea into Clive’s own toolkit: three Eikons can be equipped at once, each offering cooldown-gated abilities, freely triggered mid-combo.","mechanism":"In MDA terms, all three chase the same aesthetic, a power-fantasy spectacle, through mechanics that produce different dynamics: a found item makes a collection goal; a hidden, randomised trigger makes a moment the game controls; a cooldown-gated slot makes a build the player actively manages every fight. What changes is which part of the player’s attention is asked for in exchange for the same big moment.","effect":"A Final Fantasy VII player treats a rare summon Materia as an item to track down; a Final Fantasy XV player experiences a summon as a cinematic rescue on the game’s own schedule, not theirs; a Final Fantasy XVI player plans fights around which Eikonic abilities to slot.","compare":"Persona 5 (2016) gates its All-Out Attack the same way in every fight: only once every enemy is knocked down. Final Fantasy re-gates its summon each generation, found item, random trigger, cooldown, so the same move asks for a different skill each time.","cost":"A found summon rewards only players willing to explore for it; a randomised trigger like Final Fantasy XV’s spends the player’s agency on a moment the game controls; Final Fantasy XVI’s abilities, back after every cooldown, give up the rarity the earlier two systems traded on.","principle":"When a series keeps one spectacle move across different battle systems, treat its gating, a found item, a random trigger, a cooldown, as the actual design decision; the same button can be a discovery, a gift or a tool depending only on what it costs to press.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_VII","https://www.rpgsite.net/feature/5114-final-fantasy-xv-guide-summon-guide-how-to-summon-and-what-each-of-the-astrals-does","https://gameinformer.com/review/final-fantasy-xvi/victory-in-the-land-of-gods-and-monsters","https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/final-fantasy-16-review-on-its-own-terms/1900-6418081/"]},"ui":{"claim":"Final Fantasy’s interface has shrunk in lockstep with its battle system, from a text-and-numbers menu the player reads between actions to a HUD the player barely looks away from the fight to check.","evidence":"Final Fantasy VI’s (1994) Esper (summon) menu, in the Pixel Remaster’s layout, nests several screens deep: an Abilities tab, then an Espers-or-Magic choice, then a scrollable list of over a dozen Espers, each MP cost checked one at a time. Final Fantasy XVI (2023) shows its three equipped Eikons as icons beside the health bar and labels each button’s action at bottom right; Eikon abilities are assigned outside combat and fired with one press, with no summon menu.","mechanism":"In Fagerholt and Lorentzon’s terms both screens are non-diegetic, so the framework alone does not separate them; the change is in when the information is read. Final Fantasy VI’s menu is consulted while no fight is running, several layers deep; Final Fantasy XVI’s HUD must be read while the fight runs, so it shrinks to icons and ready-or-not states beside the action.","effect":"A Final Fantasy VI player must open the field menu between fights and navigate nested screens to check which Esper is equipped or its exact MP cost; a Final Fantasy XVI player never leaves the fight, since every action, Eikonic abilities included, fires from a button without opening a menu.","compare":"Kingdom Hearts (2002), Square’s real-time action RPG directed by Tetsuya Nomura, keeps a command menu at the bottom left that the player scrolls through mid-fight to reach magic and items; Final Fantasy XVI binds each action to its own button instead, so its bottom-right cluster is a legend to glance at, not a menu to navigate.","cost":"Final Fantasy VI’s menus give a text-literate player perfect information, exact MP costs, full spell names, at the cost of speed and immersion; Final Fantasy XVI’s icon-and-cooldown HUD is fast to read mid-fight but tells the player only ready or not, hiding exact numbers unless they leave combat to check a separate menu.","principle":"As a battle system speeds from turn-based to real-time, information has to migrate from nested, textual menus into compact, glanceable HUD states positioned right next to the action, or the system’s new speed and its old information design will fight each other.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_VI","https://gameinformer.com/review/final-fantasy-xvi/victory-in-the-land-of-gods-and-monsters","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_Hearts_(video_game)"]},"art":{"claim":"Final Fantasy has changed its chief visual author almost as often as its battle system, unlike Dragon Quest, its closest domestic rival, which kept one character designer for thirty-eight years.","evidence":"Yoshitaka Amano was the series’ conceptual, image and graphic designer from Final Fantasy (1987) until he “stepped down” after Final Fantasy VI (1994). Character design then passed to Tetsuya Nomura for VII, VIII and X, to a team including Toshiyuki Itahana for IX, Akihiko Yoshida for XII, Roberto Ferrari for XV, and Kazuya Takahashi and Hiroshi Minagawa for XVI. Dragon Quest’s Akira Toriyama and composer Koichi Sugiyama, by contrast, “have handled their respective roles on most games in the series”, Toriyama from 1986 until his death in 2024.","mechanism":"A new chief designer redraws the whole cast’s shape language rather than extending an established one, so visual continuity has to be carried by things no single designer owns: Amano still designs every entry’s title logo, and chocobos, moogles and crystals recur in whatever style the current designer draws, so the brand survives each redraw.","effect":"A player who loves Final Fantasy VI’s watercolour-styled box art meets an entirely different visual grammar in Final Fantasy VII, so loyalty to the brand cannot rest on a consistent look the way Dragon Quest’s can; the series instead teaches players to recognise it by motif and mechanic rather than by art style.","compare":"Dragon Quest kept Toriyama’s cartoonish, immediately identifiable monster and character designs across every mainline entry from 1986 until his 2024 death, so a Dragon Quest game is visually recognisable at a glance regardless of hardware generation; Final Fantasy’s one fixed image since 1994 is Amano’s title logo, not its cast.","cost":"Swapping visual authors costs continuity of tone, since Amano’s brushwork arguably suited a myth-like formality that Nomura’s more contemporary clothing choices do not, and it means the series brand cannot be defended visually the way a mascot-driven series can, shifting that job onto recurring creature and prop designs instead.","principle":"If a series’ battle system and world reset every entry by design, a rotating visual author is a matching decision, not a competing one, but budget separately for a small set of enduring creature or prop designs that can carry a visual identity no single artist’s tenure will last long enough to hold alone.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoshitaka_Amano","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Quest","https://store.steampowered.com/app/3837340/FINAL_FANTASY_VII/"]},"sound":{"claim":"Final Fantasy’s two fixed musical signatures aged differently: the Prelude, tied to the opening, outlived every reinvention, while the Victory Fanfare, tied to a battle that ends on a results screen, stopped being standard after the tenth entry, returning later only in altered forms.","evidence":"Most of the games open with the Prelude, which has evolved “from a simple, two-voice, arpeggiated theme in the early games to a complex melodic arrangement in recent installments”. Battle victories “in the first 10 installments of the series were accompanied by a victory fanfare”. Both are by Nobuo Uematsu, sole composer of the first nine mainline scores. Final Fantasy XIV: A Realm Reborn puts an arrangement titled “Prelude - Rebirth” on its title screen. The fanfare returns in altered forms: Final Fantasy XIII combines it with a “Battle Results” theme, Final Fantasy XV plays it as “Day’s End Fanfare” when the party rests at a camp or lodging, and Final Fantasy XVI plays it after certain bosses in a Gregorian-chant arrangement by Masayoshi Soken.","mechanism":"In IEZA terms the two cues sit in different layers. The Prelude is affect: non-diegetic music that sets the franchise’s mood before play starts and depends on no rule. The Victory Fanfare is interface: a non-diegetic signal that a game event, the end of a fight, has happened, so it lives only as long as that event does; arguably, once Final Fantasy XII folded battles into the field, the fanfare lost its moment.","effect":"A player who has never touched a numbered Final Fantasy game can still recognise its opening arpeggios or its post-battle jingle from clips alone, since these two motifs, not any character or plot, are what “sounds like Final Fantasy” across 36 years and several different composers.","compare":"John Williams’ Star Wars main title plays over every mainline film in that saga regardless of story, functioning as a literal, near-fixed anthem rather than a house style; Final Fantasy uses the same device twice over, an opening theme and a victory cue, stitched into an interactive medium where the fanfare must trigger correctly thousands of times per playthrough rather than once per screening.","cost":"Keeping two cues fixed while everything else resets means the series’ aural identity rests on two short pieces of music doing all the identity work other franchises spread across many recurring themes; the Victory Fanfare quietly disappeared as a fixed cue after the tenth entry, and part of that inherited identity went with it.","principle":"Tie a series’ signature cue to something every entry will keep, such as its opening, rather than to a mechanic the next entry may remove; an interface cue bound to one battle structure retires when that structure does.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Final_Fantasy","https://finalfantasy.fandom.com/wiki/Prelude_(theme)","https://finalfantasy.fandom.com/wiki/Victory_Fanfare"]},"lore":{"claim":"Final Fantasy tells the same argument, that a great power tied to crystals costs the world dear when it is misused, over and over with an entirely new cast each time, using a small kit of recurring names rather than a continuing plot to signal that a new entry is still a Final Fantasy game.","evidence":"Final Fantasy’s (1987) darkened crystals are restored only once the four Fiends are defeated; thirty-six years later, Final Fantasy XVI (2023) drives its whole war around Mothercrystals whose aether “fuels the magicks” nations rely on, while, as that aether fades, “the lifeless deadlands spread ever further”. Between them, Cid has “appeared in multiple forms”, and his “most frequent occupation is an engineer”, “commonly associated with the party’s airship”, since his debut in Final Fantasy II (1988), and chocobos have “appeared in every mainline Final Fantasy game” since that same entry.","mechanism":"In Jenkins’ terms, each entry evokes rather than embeds continuity: no returning protagonist or embedded backstory links Final Fantasy to Final Fantasy XVI, so the “world” a veteran recognises is evoked entirely through recurring furniture, not through remembered plot.","effect":"A player can start at any numbered entry with no continuity homework, since nothing assumes knowledge of an earlier game’s story, only recognition of its symbols.","compare":"The Legend of Zelda takes the opposite route to the same problem: it reuses Link and Zelda as “various incarnations” and keeps Ganon “the same person in every game” with one exception, later ordered by the official timeline in Hyrule Historia (2011); Final Fantasy discards even the names, keeping only object and role names, a crystal, an engineer called Cid, rather than any character identity across entries.","cost":"Motif-only continuity means the series banks no accumulated character loyalty the way a returning hero can build across sequels; every entry has to make a player care about a cast introduced from zero, in the same hours a returning-cast series could spend on new plot instead.","principle":"An anthology series can signal “you are in the right franchise” through a small kit of recurring objects and role-names rather than character identity, but only if that kit, not the plot, is what the opening hour and the marketing actually lean on.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_(video_game)","https://na.finalfantasyxvi.com/story","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recurring_elements_in_the_Final_Fantasy_series","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda"]},"world":{"claim":"Each numbered Final Fantasy invents a full geography, religion and technology level from nothing, an expensive habit a single persistent setting, its closest possible alternative, would remove at a stroke.","evidence":"Final Fantasy X (2001) invented Spira from scratch, including its own Yevon religion and the Al Bhed people’s distinct language and machina technology, none of it carried over from an earlier entry’s setting. Final Fantasy XV (2016) invented an entirely new world again, Eos, built as “a fantasy based on reality”, with real-world cars and petrol stations unlike any earlier entry’s default fantasy trappings, after roughly ten years of development that began in 2006 as Final Fantasy Versus XIII.","mechanism":"In Wolf’s terms, the “invention” cost of a world, its geography, culture, technology, mythology, is paid in full for almost every mainline entry rather than amortised across a shared continuity, so a Final Fantasy world-bible is drafted from zero roughly once per entry.","effect":"Veteran players cannot assume any previous game’s rules of magic, races or calendar carry over, so each entry has to teach its whole cosmology again inside its opening hours, and a returning player’s world-knowledge from an earlier setting is not currency in the next game.","compare":"The Elder Scrolls keeps one persistent continent, Tamriel, across Morrowind (2002), Oblivion (2006) and Skyrim (2011), so each new entry can spend its invention budget deepening an inherited setting instead of drafting one from scratch; Final Fantasy almost never gets that discount.","cost":"Repeated from-scratch worldbuilding is expensive in development time, arguably part of why Final Fantasy XV took roughly a decade, and it means a beloved setting is usually visited once and then retired rather than built upon by a direct sequel.","principle":"A series that resets its whole setting every entry should budget the cost of full re-invention explicitly against a persistent-setting rival’s discount, rather than treating a fresh world as free simply because worldbuilding happens every time regardless.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_X","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XV"]},"env":{"claim":"Final Fantasy has staged the price of harvesting a magical power source twice, twenty-six years apart, first as a single exception inside one city and later as a dying landscape spread across a whole continent.","evidence":"Final Fantasy VII’s (1997) Midgar runs on Mako reactors, and Shinra’s activities “drain the world of its life force, the ‘Lifestream’”; inside the city, the disused church Aerith tends is “among the few places with greenery”, and she says “you can’t grow grass and flowers in Midgar”. Final Fantasy XVI (2023) spreads the same idea across its whole map: as the Mothercrystals’ aether fades, “the lifeless deadlands spread ever further”, while nations fight over “the final flickers of the Mothers’ light”.","mechanism":"Smith and Worch’s staged cause-and-effect vignette scales up between the two games: Midgar stages the cost of Mako extraction as one legible exception, a patch of flowers in a city of steel, that a player finds on foot; Valisthea stages the same cost as an ambient, continent-wide condition the player rides through for dozens of hours, trading a readable landmark for cumulative environmental dressing.","effect":"A Midgar player learns what Mako costs from one contrast, the only flowers in the city blooming in a disused church; a Valisthea player absorbs the cost of aether use gradually, as an accumulating background fact rather than a single staged event.","compare":"Half-Life 2’s City 17 stages the cost of an occupying power inside one continuous, walkable city; Final Fantasy XVI instead spreads its environmental argument across an entire overworld map, closer to using a whole continent’s terrain to argue one thesis than a single city’s architecture.","cost":"A single-city vignette like Midgar is cheap to build once and easy to miss entirely, but lands clearly when found; a continent-wide condition like the Blight is expensive to art-direct everywhere, and risks becoming wallpaper a player stops consciously registering after a few hours.","principle":"Staging the cost of a fictional resource can scale from a single landmark to an entire overworld, but the bigger the scale, the more that argument depends on visible variation, new stages of decay, rather than a uniform, spreadable texture the eye quickly discounts as background.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midgar","https://na.finalfantasyxvi.com/story"]},"business":{"claim":"Final Fantasy has run three different business models on three different clocks at once: a premium, single-purchase mainline entry every few years, a very rare permanent switch to a monthly subscription, and, once, a full and costly rebuild of a failed live product rather than its cancellation.","evidence":"Final Fantasy XI (2002) broke from every earlier premium entry to require a monthly subscription, priced at $11.95 “since its debut in 2002”, reaching profit past 200,000 subscribers by December 2003 and becoming, by June 2012, “the most profitable title in the Final Fantasy series”. Final Fantasy XIV launched the same way in 2010, failed so badly (a 49 out of 100 Metacritic score) that Square Enix suspended its own fees, then chose in 2011 to rebuild it entirely from scratch as A Realm Reborn (2013), helping return the company to profit in fiscal year 2014 after an “extremely poor” 2013.","mechanism":"Unlike a bad premium entry, which sells fewer copies once and is forgotten, a subscription MMO’s failure compounds every month it stays broken, so Square Enix’s two bets show the model’s extremes: Final Fantasy XI’s slow, durable profit against Final Fantasy XIV’s near-total 2010 write-off, salvaged only by treating the rebuild as a new game.","effect":"Final Fantasy XI’s subscriber base let Square Enix treat a numbered entry as recurring revenue for the first time; Final Fantasy XIV’s collapse and rebuild taught the company, in Naoki Yoshida’s account, to prioritise the series’ reputation over near-term sales, visible in its growth to over 30 million registered players by January 2024.","compare":"Most premium franchises that stumble simply ship a better sequel; Square Enix instead rebuilt the same title under the same name while still live, closer to a live-service studio relaunching a failing game than how a single-purchase RPG series usually recovers from a weak entry.","cost":"The subscription model asks players for ongoing payment a premium entry never does, and the 2010-to-2013 rebuild cost Square Enix roughly three years of a large team’s time on one already-released game, time a sequel would have spent on new content.","principle":"A subscription-funded entry in an otherwise premium series can fail in a way no boxed release can, compounding every month it stays broken, so budget for a full, costed rebuild, not just a patch plan, before launching one.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XI","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XIV","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XIV_(2010_video_game)"]},"replay":{"claim":"Final Fantasy’s difficulty and replay value come almost entirely from whichever growth system that entry invented, so replaying an old entry can date badly in a way replaying its story never does.","evidence":"Final Fantasy V (1992) lets players “freely select jobs” for each character “to master” and carry mastered abilities between them, a system open enough to support the Four Job Fiesta, a yearly fan-run charity challenge, not a feature of the game, in which each player must finish with four randomly assigned jobs. Final Fantasy XVI (2023) has no job system: its replay comes from New Game Plus, a harder Final Fantasy mode with new enemy placements, an arcade mode that scores combat, and trials that limit Clive, such as to one Eikon’s powers.","mechanism":"Because each entry’s replay hook is built into that entry’s own unique growth system rather than a stable series-wide chassis, replay value is not cumulative the way a long-running RPG series’ shared engine usually makes it; a player fluent in Final Fantasy V’s job combinations gains little that transfers directly to Final Fantasy XVI’s cooldown loadouts, even though XVI’s combat was designed by turning Final Fantasy V’s mechanics into a real-time system.","effect":"A Final Fantasy V veteran can invent new challenges for themselves, a four-job-only run, decades after release, because the job system itself is the toy; a Final Fantasy XVI player replays inside challenges the designers wrote, such as a trial limited to one Eikon, rather than ones they invent.","compare":"Diablo-style action RPGs keep one shared class-and-build chassis across sequels, so replay knowledge, itemisation logic, skill trees, largely transfers between entries; Final Fantasy’s replay value resets with its systems, so it has to be reinvented, or lost, once per game rather than compounded across the franchise.","cost":"Inventing a new growth system each entry means some entries are simply less replayable than others by design, not by mistake, and a series fan cannot count on a favourite replay style, job juggling, licence-board planning, carrying into the next mainline entry at all.","principle":"When a series rebuilds its core systems every entry, replay depth has to be evaluated and marketed per game, not assumed from the franchise name; a franchise’s best replay system does not guarantee its next entry will have one at all.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_V","https://www.rpgsite.net/feature/11964-final-fantasy-v-four-job-fiesta-guide-how-to-tackle-this-unique-challenge","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_XVI"]},"lineage":{"claim":"Final Fantasy’s Active Time Battle system escaped its own series within four years, travelling not to a rival studio but to a same-company, cross-franchise team Square explicitly built to mix its own talent with its biggest domestic competitor’s.","evidence":"Hiroyuki Ito designed the Active Time Battle system for Final Fantasy IV (1991); by 1995, Chrono Trigger reused it directly as “Active Time Battle 2.0”, built by Square’s self-styled “Dream Team” of Final Fantasy creator Hironobu Sakaguchi, Dragon Quest creator Yuji Horii and Dragon Quest character designer Akira Toriyama. Decades later, Final Fantasy VII Remake (2020) kept the original game’s ATB mechanics “in a reversed way”, pausing once a gauge fills to choose a special action from a menu, folding the same 1991 invention into an action game.","mechanism":"This is lineage by credit rather than outside imitation: Chrono Trigger names its system “Active Time Battle 2.0”, openly versioning a Final Fantasy mechanic inside another Square game, and the series later redesigned the same system for itself, showing a mechanism can be transformed twice, real-time to turn order to real-time-with-pause, and still trace back to one 1991 invention.","effect":"A player who learns the Active Time Battle rhythm, plan during downtime, act when the gauge fills, in one Square RPG carries that literacy into an unrelated Square RPG built years later, and Final Fantasy VII Remake players effectively relearn the same underlying rhythm dressed as a modern action game.","compare":"Dragon Quest, Chrono Trigger’s other parent series, never adopted Active Time Battle itself and instead kept a strictly turn-based system across its own mainline entries; the gauge stayed a Final Fantasy-lineage feature even while its designers briefly shared a studio and a project.","cost":"Reusing a named mechanic across sibling franchises risked making Chrono Trigger read as Final Fantasy with a different cast to some players, trading a fully original battle system for a faster, better-tested one. The mechanic was also protected: Ito and Hironobu Sakaguchi held an Active Time Battle patent from 1995 until it expired in 2010.","principle":"A mechanic can outlive the genre it was built for if its rhythm is kept rather than its interface: plan while a gauge fills, act when it is full, fits inside turns and inside real-time action alike.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Active_Time_Battle","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrono_Trigger"]}},"shots":[{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-eikon-battle.webp","lens":"gameplay","alt":"Final Fantasy XVI: Clive fights an enemy in a stone courtyard at night, casting Thunder as lightning arcs across the screen, with the Thunder, Attack, Jump and Blind Justice button labels at bottom right.","caption":"Final Fantasy XVI’s HUD: the three equipped Eikons sit beside the health bar, and each button’s action, including Ramuh’s Blind Justice, is labelled at bottom right.","callouts":[{"x":0.8,"y":0.76,"t":"Each button’s action, labelled: no menu to open"},{"x":0.06,"y":0.27,"t":"Three equipped Eikons: Ramuh active, two in reserve"}],"credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/2515020/FINAL_FANTASY_XVI/","licence":"store"}},{"img":"assets/games/shots/final-fantasy-menu-ui.webp","lens":"ui","alt":"Final Fantasy VI Pixel Remaster: the Abilities menu’s Espers list open on Celes, showing named Espers including Ramuh, Ifrit and Cait Sith, with the selected Esper’s effect and MP cost below.","caption":"Final Fantasy VI’s Esper menu: a named, scrollable list read outside battle, several menu layers deep.","callouts":[{"x":0.46,"y":0.4,"t":"Cait Sith, the equipped Esper, ticked in the list"},{"x":0.56,"y":0.19,"t":"MP and character portrait, checked one at a time"}],"credit":{"author":"Square Enix","url":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1173820/","licence":"store"}}]},"abb35ead");
